%S A400843 3,17,19,31,113,127,131,157,179,223,227,229,241,251,257,281,293,331,
%T A400843 349,421,467,479,563,571,593,641,647,673,823,859,907,911,1013,1039,
%U A400843 1091,1103,1171,1217,1249,1319,1439,1471,1511,1531,1549,1571,1663,1697,1721,1733,1777,1933,2003,2063,2083,2153,2161,2267,2287
%N A400843 Primes p for which there exists a prime q <= p for which 2*((p#/p) / (q#/q)) == -1 mod p, where p# = A034386(p).
%C A400843 After the initial 3, primes p for which exists a prime q < p, such that A276085(p^2 * q) is a multiple of p. Proof: for p>q, A276085(p^2 * q) = 2*(p#/p) + (q#/q) = (1 + 2*((p#/p)/(q#/q))) * (q#/q), and as the latter factor certainly is not a multiple of p, the first factor must be, if we want the whole expression to be a multiple of p.
%C A400843 Conjectured to be the union A400842 U {17, 241}. See comments in A401053 and in A401054.
%H A400843 Antti Karttunen, <a href="/A400843/b400843.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..3800</a>
%H A400843 <a href="/index/Pri#primorial_numbers">Index entries for sequences related to primorial numbers</a>.
%F A400843 {Primes p such that A401053(A000720(p)-1) > 0}.
%e A400843 3 is a term because 1 + 2*((3#/3) / (q#/q)) will be a multiple of 3 when we set also q to 3.
%e A400843 17 is a term because 1 + 2*((17#/17) / (q#/q)) will be a multiple of 17 when we set q to 2, as then (1+(2*A002110(A000720(17)-1)/A002110(A000720(2)-1))) = 1+2*A002110(6) = 60061 = 17*3533.
%e A400843 19 is a term because 1 + 2*((19#/19) / (q#/q)) will be a multiple of 19 when we set q to 3, in which case the formula reduces [because 3#/3 = 2] to 1 + 17# = 1+A002110(7), which is a multiple of 19, as 510511 = 19*97*277.
%o A400843 (PARI) is_A400843_on_p(p) = { my(n=primepi(p)-1, pr=2); forstep(k=n,1,-1,if((p-1)==pr, return(1)); pr *= prime(k); pr %= p); ((p-1)==pr); };
%o A400843 forprime(p=2,2287,if(is_A400843_on_p(p),print1(p,", ")));
%Y A400843 Cf. A000720, A276085, A401053, A401054, A401062 (gives the corresponding prime q, the largest of them, if several exist).
%Y A400843 Cf. A400842 (subsequence).
%Y A400843 Cf. also A401060, A401061, A401063 and A401057 for similar sequences.
%K A400843 nonn,new
%O A400843 1,1
%A A400843 _Antti Karttunen_, Oct 17 2026
